          <ul class="space-y-4">
            {seasons.map(([when, what]) => (
              <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
                <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
                <strong class="text-ink font-medium">{when}:</strong> {what}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Altitude note:</strong> Leh sits at 3,500 m
            (11,550 ft) and most itineraries climb from there. Fly-in travelers need two easy
            acclimatization days minimum — we build them in, no exceptions. This matters doubly if
            Hanle and Umling La are on your route: you'd be going from 3,500 m to nearly 5,800 m
            (11,550 to 19,000 ft), and that is not a trip to rush. The happy accident is that those
            acclimatization days are exactly when your permit gets sorted, so the paperwork costs
            you no trip time at all.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------- Getting the Permit Right ---------- */}
  <section class="bg-paper py-24 md:py-36">
    <div class="max-w-8xl mx-auto px-6 md:px-14">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <div class="eyebrow mb-5">— Pitfalls</div>
        <h2 class="display text-4xl md:text-5xl mb-8">Getting the Permit Right</h2>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-8">
          For tourist-visa travelers the Protected Area Permit is close to a non-event when an
          operator handles it: passport and visa copies, photos, arranged in Leh during
          acclimatization. Where travelers actually get burned:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-5">
          <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Believing the internet about Hanle and Umling
            La.</strong> The most common mistake is now the opposite of what it used to be — people
            rule out the far southeast because an old page said foreigners are barred, and never ask.
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Assuming Alchi and Lamayuru need nothing.</strong>
            They're Sham Valley. Fine on days one and two, permit from day three, and plenty of
            itineraries put them later in the trip.
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Treating Mig La as bookable.</strong> It needs army
            clearance against your dates, requested ahead.
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Arriving on an employment or business visa</strong>
            without having filed on e-FRRO weeks earlier — that's days lost at a Leh police station.
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Assuming a phone screenshot is a permit.</strong>
            Checkpoints take paper copies. Carry four.
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-[0.7em] w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-saffron"></span>
            <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Asking for an inner line permit as a
            foreigner.</strong> It's the local shorthand, but your document is a Protected Area
            Permit — ask for that and you'll get a straight answer.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

